## Introduction

Before I started this lab I understood that network monitoring was important, but I had not fully appreciated *why* until I imagined a realistic scenario. It is 2:00 a.m., a critical server has silently gone offline, and the first indication of a problem is an angry customer who cannot access a service. The outage has already affected the business, and the IT team is reacting instead of preventing the problem. An effective monitoring solution is designed to avoid exactly this.

As I learned more about enterprise networking, I realised that monitoring is not simply about displaying graphs or collecting statistics. It provides continuous visibility into the health, performance, and availability of network devices and servers. More importantly, it alerts administrators when something begins to fail, so issues can be investigated and resolved before users are affected.

### Why PRTG Network Monitor

I chose **PRTG Network Monitor**, developed by Paessler AG, for these reasons:

- It is one of the most widely used infrastructure monitoring platforms in the industry and combines enterprise-level capability with a relatively straightforward deployment.
- It supports a wide range of monitoring technologies: SNMP, WMI, NetFlow, packet sniffing, SSH, HTTP, VMware, Hyper-V, Active Directory, SQL databases, cloud services, and more.
- The **free edition supports up to 100 sensors**, which is enough to build a realistic monitoring environment while learning the platform.
- It lets me practise many of the same techniques used in production enterprise networks.

### Scope of this lab

I documented every stage of the deployment as I completed it. I installed PRTG on a Windows Server virtual machine in VMware Workstation, secured the installation, and then configured monitoring for four types of infrastructure:

- a Cisco Catalyst switch
- a Synology NAS
- a WatchGuard firewall
- a Windows Server (Domain Controller)

Along the way I worked with SNMP versions 2c and 3, WMI, LDAP, and NetFlow. All work was done on a personal computer with an Intel Core i5, 8 GB of RAM, a 256 GB SSD, Windows 11, VMware Workstation, and Windows Server 2022. No enterprise hardware was required beyond the virtual infrastructure and the devices being monitored.

By the end I had a functioning monitoring environment capable of tracking network performance, server health, storage utilisation, authentication services, and traffic flows in real time.

