## Understanding the 100-Sensor Limit

One of the first things I needed to understand was how PRTG's licensing model works. I initially assumed the free edition allowed monitoring of up to 100 *devices*. In reality **the limit is based on sensors, not devices**.

A **sensor** is a single monitoring metric on a device. If I monitor a server's CPU usage, memory utilisation, disk capacity, network traffic, and uptime, I am already using **five sensors** for that single server.

The same principle applies to network equipment. If I monitor every interface on a 48-port switch individually, I could consume more than half of the available sensors on that one device.

| Scenario | Sensors consumed |
|---|---|
| Server: CPU, memory, disk capacity, network traffic, uptime | 5 |
| 48-port switch with one traffic sensor per port | 48 (before CPU, memory, ping, uptime) |

> [!NOTE]
> Understanding this distinction changed how I planned the lab. Instead of accepting every sensor that auto-discovery proposed, I chose the most valuable sensors for each device. For example, I deleted low-value Disk Free sensors for static system partitions on the firewall ([Document 07](07-watchguard-firewall-snmpv3.md#step-5-review-the-sensors)).

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Production recommendation:** In a commercial deployment, sensor count drives licensing cost. Defining a standard "sensor set" per device type (for example, ping, CPU, memory, uptime, and uplink traffic for every switch) keeps both cost and alert noise under control.

